.TH _CLOG_WRITE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
_clog_write \- internal log-line writer (use the log_* macros instead)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <clogger.h>

void _clog_write(clog logger, clog_level_t level,
                 const char *file, int line, const char *func,
                 bool with_backtrace, const char *fmt, ...)
    __attribute__((format(printf, 7, 8)));
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Formats and emits a single structured log line. This is the function
that all log_* macros expand to; application code should call the
macros rather than this function directly.

The write sequence (all steps under the per-logger mutex) is:

  1. Check level >= min_level. If filtered AND level != CLOG_FATAL,
     return immediately (no output). CLOG_FATAL bypasses this check.
  2. Format the user message with vsnprintf into a 1024-byte stack
     buffer; spill to heap if the formatted text exceeds 1024 bytes.
     If the heap spill itself cannot be allocated, the message falls
     back to the truncated stack copy with its final bytes replaced by
     a visible "...[truncated]" marker rather than being emitted as if
     it were the complete message.
  3. If time rotation is enabled, check and fire if due.
  4. Build the log record according to the shared backing-store format:

     CLOG_FMT_LOGFMT:
       ts=<ISO-8601-UTC> level=<L> proc=<name>(<pid>):<tname>(<tid>)
       src=<file>:<line> func=<func> [key=value ...] msg=<message>

     CLOG_FMT_JSON:
       {"ts":"...","level":"...","proc":"name(pid):tname(tid)",
        "src":"file:N","func":"...", [fields,] "msg":"..." [,"bt":[...]]}
       The "bt" array is appended inline when with_backtrace is true.
       If the array cannot be built (backtrace_symbols() failure, or a
       buffer-growth allocation failure), a "bt_error":"unavailable"
       key is written in its place instead, so the record still closes
       normally and the omission is never silent. Every string value
       (message and every field key/value) is validated as UTF-8
       before being embedded, since JSON text must be valid Unicode;
       a byte or byte sequence that is not well-formed UTF-8 is
       replaced, one invalid byte at a time, with the Unicode
       replacement character (U+FFFD).

     CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG:
       <PRI>1 TIMESTAMP HOSTNAME APP-NAME PID MSGID
       [ccol proc="..." src="..." func="..." [fields]] MSG
       Control characters (including newline and carriage return) in
       MSG and in every proc/src/func/field value are backslash-
       escaped, so a single record can never span more than one line.
       HOSTNAME and APP-NAME are cached at logger-construction time
       filtered to RFC 5424 PRINTUSASCII (0x21-0x7e), so neither can
       contain a byte that would need this same escaping.

     Every value above is escaped or quoted per the target format's
     own rules; if building the full record would exceed the write
     buffer's hard cap (see below), the partial content is discarded
     and replaced with a short, well-formed placeholder record instead.

  5. Write the record atomically with write(2) via an EINTR-retry loop.
  6. If with_backtrace is true, emit the backtrace:
     CLOG_FMT_LOGFMT: call backtrace() + backtrace_symbols() and
       write each frame as a tab-indented continuation line:
         \et#N symbol\en
     CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG: emit each frame as a separate RFC 5424 syslog
       message (same PRI, TIMESTAMP, and MSGID as the original record).
     CLOG_FMT_JSON: backtrace was already embedded as "bt" in step 4.
     In both CLOG_FMT_LOGFMT and CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG, the symbol text for
     each frame is backslash-escaped the same way MSG is, so an
     unusual symbol name can never split a frame's own line into two;
     a frame whose line cannot be built (buffer-growth allocation
     failure, or too little room left before the internal buffer cap)
     is skipped in favor of any shorter frame that follows, rather
     than written incomplete or discarding the whole backtrace, so
     every line that is written is always a complete, well-formed
     record. If capture failed entirely (backtrace() unsupported on
     this platform, or backtrace_symbols() itself failed to allocate),
     or every individual frame failed to fit, a single fixed
     "backtrace unavailable" marker line (CLOG_FMT_LOGFMT) or marker
     record (CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG) is emitted in place of the missing
     per-frame output, mirroring CLOG_FMT_JSON's own
     "bt_error":"unavailable" key, so the omission is never silent.
  7. If size rotation is enabled, check and fire if due.
  8. If level == CLOG_FATAL, call exit(EXIT_FAILURE).

When the logger was created with a non-NULL clog_async_cfg_t (see
clog_open_fd_mp(3)) and level != CLOG_FATAL, steps 3-7 above run on a
dedicated writer thread instead of the calling thread: this function
only formats the message and captures a snapshot of the logger's
current fields, timestamp, and thread identity (steps 1-2, plus the
backtrace capture for step 6, all still done here so they reflect the
calling thread rather than the writer thread), then hands the rest
off to be aggregated with records from other handles sharing the
same target and written later. CLOG_FATAL always runs the full
sequence synchronously on the calling thread regardless of async
configuration, after first draining anything already queued from
earlier, non-fatal calls.

The write buffer is a per-logger dynamic buffer that starts at 4 KiB
and grows (doubling) up to a 16 MiB hard cap. It is reused across
calls. If a single record (message, structured fields, and framing
combined) would exceed that cap, the oversized content is discarded
and a short, well-formed placeholder record is written in its place,
in the same output format, noting the original message's size in
bytes; this guarantees a record can never be emitted truncated
mid-value or missing its closing delimiters or trailing newline. The
same placeholder also covers a transient, size-unrelated allocation
failure while enumerating structured fields; its note reports the
allocation failure directly rather than misattributing it to size.
For CLOG_FMT_JSON, if with_backtrace is true the placeholder still
carries a "bt_error":"unavailable" key, exactly like a "bt" array
that failed to embed inline, so a requested backtrace is never
silently indistinguishable from one that was never requested. In the
extreme case where memory pressure is severe enough that not even
this placeholder record can be built, a minimal, still
format-appropriate diagnostic line is written directly instead, so a
record is never reduced to zero written bytes with no trace of it
anywhere in the output.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
logger          Logger handle.
level           Severity level of this message.
file            Source file name (__FILE__).
line            Source line number (__LINE__).
func            Function name (__func__).
with_backtrace  If true, append a stack backtrace after the log line.
fmt             printf-style format string.
\&...             Format arguments.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Backtrace support requires a platform with execinfo.h (glibc, macOS,
FreeBSD) and the binary linked with -rdynamic for symbol resolution.
On other platforms, or on a transient allocation failure while
resolving symbol names, capture itself fails, but the omission is
never silent (see step 6 above).
The format attribute causes the compiler to type-check fmt and the
variadic arguments at compile time.
Asserts if logger is CLOG_INVALID, stale, or already closed.
.fi
